{
  "id": "next-greater-node-in-linked-list",
  "questionId": "next-greater-node-in-linked-list",
  "slug": "next-greater-node-in-linked-list",
  "title": "Next Greater Node in Linked List",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Linked list",
  "patterns": [
    "linked list",
    "monotonic stack"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "the brute force rescans the rest of the list for every node - can earlier nodes 'remember' that they're still waiting for a greater value?",
    "what if you kept a stack of indices whose next-greater value you haven't found yet?",
    "as you scan left to right, whenever the current value is greater than the value at the index on top of the stack, that stack index has found its answer - pop it and record the current value",
    "keep popping and recording while the current value beats the stack top, then push the current index (it's now waiting for its own next greater value); anything left on the stack at the end gets 0"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using greater-or-equal instead of strictly greater, which incorrectly matches equal values",
    "pushing values onto the stack instead of indices, losing the ability to write results back to the correct position",
    "forgetting to default unresolved stack entries to 0 at the end"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]` is the value of the i-th node in order.\n\n**For each node, find the value of the first node that comes after it in the list with a strictly greater value, using 0 if no such node exists, and return these results as an array in the same order as the original list.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^4",
      "1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node list",
      "strictly decreasing values (every result is 0)",
      "strictly increasing values (every result is the next value except the last, which is 0)",
      "duplicate values (a next greater node must be strictly greater, so equal values don't count)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [2, 1, 5]",
        "output": "[5, 5, 0]",
        "expectedOutput": "[5, 5, 0]",
        "explanation": "node 0 (value 2) and node 1 (value 1) both find node 2 (value 5) as their next greater node; node 2 has nothing after it"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [2, 7, 4, 3, 5]",
        "output": "[7, 0, 5, 5, 0]",
        "expectedOutput": "[7, 0, 5, 5, 0]",
        "explanation": "value 2's next greater is 7; value 7 has nothing greater after it; both 4 and 3 find 5 later on"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1]",
        "output": "[0]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0]",
        "explanation": "a single node has nothing after it"
